{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-41702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the $x$-axis, for $\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi .$ Express ${\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-41702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/264cb888010525848f647669653d34383535dd32e6ac264927105540c3b85f9f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-(x-y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the Green's Theorem we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-41702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7f70f1753c875013d6339c1e9750cc904df70ebe16fee2ede0e723e0a57c7be"} {"question" "q-41702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-41702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(x+y)dy\\,dx"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}(x-y)dy\\,dx"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}x\\,dy\\,dx"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-41702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,2),(1,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/8e3efc3f9c58fb0f9a84144e97a300b6ab2a4011d47ee0d2bae1edc8a3f721cd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7f70f1753c875013d6339c1e9750cc904df70ebe16fee2ede0e723e0a57c7be"} {"question" "q-109130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}-{y}^{2},{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square with vertices at $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ $(1,1)$ and $(-1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/574172dda3031b9bb44dbcfb46265453ae543a75bccf8db24e93625e7154bba9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2xy+{y}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x(1)+(1)^{2}-2x(-1)-(-1)^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4x\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[{x}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7f70f1753c875013d6339c1e9750cc904df70ebe16fee2ede0e723e0a57c7be"} {"question" "q-109151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2x+{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/fafd281fc63fb276c9759fd84f722096d494aa3e3fe9441f43aa6ecd1f9dfda9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2xy-{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{9})dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{45}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{4}}{6}-\\frac{(3)^{5}}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7f70f1753c875013d6339c1e9750cc904df70ebe16fee2ede0e723e0a57c7be"} {"question" "q-109166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{27}{15}"] ["math" "-\\frac{64}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{81}{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{81}{10}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y$ and $x=4y-{y}^{2}.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/239867aec8cff7840667d2f4eafa9bc97d4fc5af09b684d863fef06fd8fd28ad.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 4y-{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a7f70f1753c875013d6339c1e9750cc904df70ebe16fee2ede0e723e0a57c7be"} {"question" "q-109249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{3y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}(2x{e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"} {"question" "q-109249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d94199f74082fe9faace740bb692687ec1e5069532784c2490f4e7ee1ec7a5e"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2170 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
